resources/azkar.json
-----------------------------
Source: Morning and Evening Adhkar Database
        https://github.com/Seen-Arabic/Morning-And-Evening-Adhkar-DB
        Taken at commit 29d7623fede52eca835a789025dfda866e8cfe44
License: MIT

Provides the morning and evening adhkar shown by this application, in
Arabic with an English translation and transliteration.

Note on what is licensed: the Arabic text is Qur'an and hadith and is not
subject to copyright. The MIT licence covers the compilation, the English
translation and the transliteration. Before this data was adopted, its
Arabic was checked against nine well-known adhkar - Ayat al-Kursi, Sayyid
al-Istighfar, al-Ikhlas, al-Falaq, an-Nas, "Bismillah alladhi la yadurru",
"Radeetu billahi rabban", "Hasbiyallah" and "Subhanallah wa bihamdihi" -
all of which are present and correctly attributed.

Changes made: reduced to the fields this application uses, the numeric
"type" field translated into a readable morning/evening/both value, and
re-serialised. Each entry's original hadith citation is carried through
unchanged so any text can be checked against its own reference. The
conversion is reproducible with tools/make-azkar.mjs.

Quran translations (downloaded at the user's request; NOT bundled)
-----------------------------
Source: Tanzil Project — https://tanzil.net/trans/

No translation of the Quran is distributed with this application. When a
user chooses a translation in Settings, the application downloads it
directly from Tanzil to that user's own machine.

This is deliberate. Tanzil's translations page states that the
translations there are "for non-commercial purposes only". A
non-commercial restriction is not an open-source licence, so including one
in this GPL-3.0 application — or in its source repository, or in its
signed installer — is not permissible. Downloading on request keeps the
project from redistributing anything: the user obtains the text themselves
for their own non-commercial use.

Tanzil ask that applications using more than three of their translations
link back to https://tanzil.net/trans/ so users can track updates. The
application does so in Settings, beside the translation list.

The Arabic Quran text bundled with this application is a separate matter:
it is licensed CC-BY 3.0 and is documented at the top of this file.
